;; The built-in settings. This file is read from the classpath, so in a
;; packaged jar it lives inside the jar: an installation puts its own paths
;; and limits in a file of its own, named by CORPUS_PROBE_CONFIG or the
;; corpus-probe.config system property, and that is merged over this one.
;; See README.md.

;; A port of its own, deliberately not 8080 or 3000: those are where every
;; other dev server on a machine already is, and a corpus server that has
;; to be moved before it will start is one more thing to remember.
{:port        7373
 :registry    "dev/corpus/registry"
 ;; locale for CQP's ExternalSort collation (needs gawk on PATH) and for the
 ;; app's own collation of metadata and frequency values, so the two agree;
 ;; all KU corpora are Danish, so a single locale suffices for now.
 :sort-locale "da_DK.UTF-8"
 ;; Where CQP saves query results, so that paging or re-sorting a search
 ;; does not run it again; drop :cache-dir to query afresh every time.
 ;;
 ;; A saved result holds the whole result, not the page: 12 bytes per
 ;; match sorted and 8 unsorted, so a whole-corpus result of the largest
 ;; KU corpus (MEMO_ALL, 64.6M tokens) is 739 MiB sorted, and each of the
 ;; five sort modes is a separate file. :cache-max-bytes must therefore be
 ;; a good multiple of the largest single result: below it, results from
 ;; that corpus can never be kept at all. The least recently read go
 ;; first, and reaping runs only when the server is working, so this is a
 ;; high-water mark rather than an average.
 :cache-dir       "dev/cache"
 :cache-ttl-ms    1200000
 :cache-max-bytes 10737418240
 ;; how many cqp processes one request runs at once
 :parallelism 8
 ;; A cqp process is killed after :timeout-ms, which covers reading a page
 ;; out of a result already saved: milliseconds. Running the query is a
 ;; different order of magnitude and gets :query-timeout-ms. Counting is
 ;; running the query too, so it gets the same one: less would let a
 ;; corpus time out while being counted and succeed while being shown,
 ;; and a count that fails is left out of the total, costing the search
 ;; pages of hits it has.
 ;;
 ;; Measured on a 64.6M token corpus, the size of the largest at KU:
 ;; counting all of it 2.4 s, a page in corpus order 2.3 s and 0.2 s once
 ;; saved, but sorting every match 650 s by word and over 13 minutes by
 ;; left context, at 3 to 4 GB of memory and a 1 to 2 GB temp file in
 ;; /tmp. Two million matches, which is nearer a real query, sort in 3 s
 ;; and 19 s.
 ;;
 ;; Five minutes therefore leaves about ten times the headroom a real
 ;; search needs while refusing to sort a whole large corpus, which is
 ;; deliberate. Corpus order and random sorting are internal to CQP and
 ;; unaffected. Refusing is not free: CQP leaves its temp file behind
 ;; when killed, so /tmp wants sizing, and a private one, at deployment.
 ;;
 ;; :search-budget-ms caps the whole search, and no single batch may
 ;; outlive it, so it is also the longest a request can take. A reverse
 ;; proxy needs a read timeout above it (nginx proxy_read_timeout and
 ;; Apache ProxyTimeout both default to 60 s).
 :timeout-ms       60000
 :query-timeout-ms 300000
 :search-budget-ms 300000
 ;; the corpus index grouping: folders may nest and name corpora by their
 ;; uppercase CQP ID; corpora not named anywhere are listed ungrouped, so
 ;; this tree only ever adds structure, never hides a corpus. PROBE is left
 ;; out deliberately to exercise the ungrouped tail.
 :folders     [{:label   "Litteratur"
                :folders [{:label   "Folkeviser"
                           :corpora ["VISER"]}]}
               {:label   "Folketinget"
                :corpora ["TALER"]}]}
